docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T11.md:12:This task introduces the Rust type system for the append-only event log substrate. It defines the `EventKind` enum covering all 20 spec event types, the shared `EventEnvelope` struct carrying workspace id, branch, session id, task id, actor, timestamp, stable references, payload hash, compact summary, and optional full payload location, plus the typed payload structs for each kind. Without these types, Phase 2 storage, writer, reader, and capture work has nothing to serialize. Every later phase (memory consolidation, verification, retrieval, metrics) depends on these typed records, so getting the model right now prevents downstream churn.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T11.md:20:> "The full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation."
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T62.md:12:This task wires automatic workflow-outcome recording into every redesigned tool from T56–T60 and ships the composition test suite that proves the new tools compose correctly over the underlying substrates. Spec §Phase 8 Deliverables names "workflow outcome recording integrated by default" as a binding deliverable. Spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles requires "every workflow tool should record inputs, resolved anchors, selected candidates, excluded high-scoring candidates where useful, response summary, downstream use events." Without this task, individual tools work in isolation but the event log + memory consolidation cannot observe whether a workflow succeeded, which breaks Phase 6 procedure extraction and Phase 9 success metrics.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T62.md:18:> "Every workflow tool should record: inputs; resolved anchors; selected candidates; excluded high-scoring candidates where useful; response summary; downstream use events." (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T62.md:42:2. Implement `workflow_v2/outcome_capture.rs` as a reusable interceptor: a `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` type that wraps any tool handler, captures inputs, resolved anchors, selected candidates, excluded candidates (the top-N high-scoring candidates that were rejected and why), response summary, and emits a `WorkflowSucceeded` or `WorkflowFailed` event on completion. The recorder must run on the hot path within the spec §Phase 2 budget (≤5ms P99 overhead).
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T62.md:43:3. Wire `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` into every redesigned tool from T56 and T57 by default. Wire it into every memory tool from T58/T59/T60 that participates in a user-facing workflow (excluding `get_event_trace`, which would create a feedback loop). Default-on means the tool registration in `mcp.rs` constructs the recorder around the handler; the legacy explicit `record_workflow_outcome` tool remains available as a manual override per the T02 compatibility policy.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T62.md:46:6. In `workflow_v2/composition_tests.rs`, write composition tests that exercise the new tools end-to-end against a fixture workspace: (a) `prepare_change → plan_edit → apply_patch_simulated → record outcome` chain emits the correct sequence of events; (b) `get_context_capsule → expand_context` chain returns stable handle deltas without redundant payload; (c) `diagnose_failure → prepare_change` chain reuses anchors from the failure diagnosis; (d) `save_memory → verify_explain_memory` chain transitions an unverified memory to a verified state and emits the matching events; (e) `consolidate_session → propose_memory_evolution(action="apply")` chain applies a proposal that the session produced; (f) every workflow chain emits exactly one `WorkflowSucceeded` or `WorkflowFailed` event per outer call; (g) excluded high-scoring candidates appear in the recorded outcome with rejection reasons.
