docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T06.md:12:This task introduces the unified identity type system that every later substrate (event log, memory graph, working memory, retrieval, verification) depends on. Spec §Phase 1 states "Identity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation." The task delivers stable, encodable, decodable, hashable id types for each of the seven identity kinds plus the encoding/decoding rules that make them compact in MCP payloads. The existing `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` is generalized into a common identity model.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T06.md:18:> "Definition of done: every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity; ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches; identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls."
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T28.md:7:**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine), [§Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T28.md:12:This task delivers steps 2 and 3 of the spec's retrieval pipeline: extract every literal anchor a user statement provides — file paths, symbol names, error strings, shell commands, API names, config keys — and resolve each into a stable graph identity using the Phase 1 identity resolver. Anchor resolution is the deterministic core of the pipeline; everything downstream (candidate retrieval, scoring, expansion handles) relies on the resolver returning canonical identities. Ambiguity must be surfaced, not hidden — the resolver returns the candidate set with disambiguation reasons so the scorer in T30 can weigh them and the diagnostic mode in T30/T36 can explain why each candidate was kept.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T28.md:18:> "Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: ... anchor extractor and resolver; hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events ..." (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T28.md:30:  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs` (T27) — supplies the `IntentClassification` input.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T28.md:37:3. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/anchors.rs`: pure-function extractor `extract_anchors(task_text: &str, intent: &IntentClassification) -> Vec<RawAnchor>` and resolver `resolve_anchors(raws: Vec<RawAnchor>, resolver: &IdentityResolver) -> Vec<ResolvedAnchor>`. Resolution returns the canonical identity, the candidate set when ambiguous, the ambiguity reason, and the source span in the task text.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T28.md:40:6. Emit a `diagnostics()` view on `ResolvedAnchor` returning `(anchor_text, resolved_identity, ambiguity_reason, source_span)` so the scorer (T30) can apply `exact identifier match` weighting and the diagnostic mode can show why an anchor was matched or skipped.
