docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T67.md:1:# T67 — Phase 9 — get_memory_metrics + retrieval relevance surfaces wired through MCP
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T67.md:12:This task wires the T65 metrics module through the MCP surface so the `get_memory_metrics` tool from T60 returns the full canonical signal set rather than the fallback session_metrics view, and adds per-call retrieval relevance surfaces (inclusion reasons, scoring breakdowns) into the existing workflow tool responses. Spec §Phase 9 Deliverables names "retrieval relevance metrics" as binding, and spec §Phase 4 Definition of Done requires "workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles … irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed." Without this task, the metrics module ships but no MCP client can read it and no per-call relevance signal is exposed.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T67.md:16:> "Phase 9 Deliverables: retrieval relevance metrics; memory usefulness metrics; context token efficiency metrics; stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics."
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T67.md:20:> "The ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result." (spec §Retrieval Engine)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T67.md:35:2. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/metrics_surface.rs` as the MCP-facing facade over the T65 `MetricsCollector`. It exposes: (a) `MetricsSurface::collect(scope, signals)` calling the T65 collector and returning `Vec<MetricValue>`; (b) `MetricsSurface::collect_for_call(task_id, tool_name)` returning per-call relevance: per-pivot inclusion score breakdown (graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, evidence strength, past usefulness — per spec §Retrieval Engine "Ranking signals"), per-memory inclusion reason, excluded high-scoring candidates with rejection reasons; (c) `MetricsSurface::summarize_for_compact_mode(...)` returning a one-line digest suitable for compact tool responses.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T67.md:37:4. Extend the T56/T57 workflow tools' responses to attach per-call relevance via `MetricsSurface::collect_for_call`. In `compact` render mode each `MemoryHighlight` and `Pivot` carries the summarized one-line digest; in `full` / `diagnostic` mode each carries the full score breakdown. Spec §Retrieval Engine: "The ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode."
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T73.md:72:- All ID inputs use `SearchableSelect` (webview) or `vscode.window.showQuickPick` (tree-view) — proposal selection inside the dialog uses `<dialog>` content, not a separate ID picker; if cross-references to other memories or events are picked, use `showQuickPick`, never raw `<select>` or `prompt`.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T20.md:12:This task makes memory links, evidence, access history, and usefulness scores first-class records rather than columns or JSON blobs on the `memories` table. Each becomes its own table with typed Rust APIs that the rest of Phase 3 (CRUD, streams, migration, tests) and later phases (verification, retrieval, consolidation) build on. The spec is explicit that link records must carry source, target, type, strength, reason, evidence event, creator, timestamps, and verification status — none of which are expressible as denormalized columns. Without this task, contradiction and supersession cannot be queried as graph edges, retrieval cannot explain inclusion, and verification cannot recheck individual evidence anchors.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T20.md:37:5. In `memory_graph/accesses.rs`, define `pub struct MemoryAccess { access_id: MemoryAccessId, memory_id: MemoryId, accessed_at: DateTime<Utc>, accessed_in_event: EventId, accessor: Actor, inclusion_reason: String, was_used: Option<bool>, downstream_outcome_event: Option<EventId> }`. `was_used` is filled in later by retrieval-outcome correlation; nullable on initial write.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T20.md:39:7. Extend `memory_graph/schema.sql` (or add per-table SQL files included by the migrator) with:
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R38.md:12:This review certifies that Phase 5 — Working Memory — is complete, spec-faithful, and ships the excluded-context auditability that makes working memory trustworthy. The reviewer reads every file produced by T34–T37, runs the verification commands, and writes a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R38-working-memory.md`. The report uses the headings required by the verification commands so the build harness can confirm it was actually written, not stubbed. Verdict is one of `Pass`, `Pass with follow-ups`, or `Fail with required rework`; `Fail` blocks the Phase 6 consolidation work from starting.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R38.md:16:> "Working memory contains: task statement; interpreted intent; active files and symbols; active hypotheses; active failures; current plan; selected memories; excluded memories and reasons; budget decisions; unresolved questions; verification status." (spec §5. Working Memory)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R38.md:20:> "Phase 5: Working Memory — Deliverables: explicit per-task working memory state; operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint; MCP surface for inspecting current working memory; automatic event capture for included and excluded context; tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore. Definition of done: workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded; context management is no longer just prompt accumulation." (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)
