docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T37.md:18:> "Definition of done: workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded; context management is no longer just prompt accumulation." (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T37.md:36:2. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/budget_tests.rs` covering: `compress` honors `budget_decisions.token_cap`; over-budget retrieval truncates and sets `BudgetReport::truncated = true`; truncated results move to `excluded_memories` with reason `"compressed: budget=<n> tokens"`; budget changes between operations affect only subsequent operations, not retroactively; a synthetic huge candidate set respects per-source caps so working memory never grows unbounded.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T37.md:37:3. Implement pinned-context regression tests inside `budget_tests.rs` (or split into a small `pin_tests` if file size demands): `pin` makes an identity survive `compress`; `pin` twice on the same identity is idempotent; `evict` on a pinned identity without `force=true` is refused with a clear error; `evict` with `force=true` records the pinned identity in `excluded_memories` with reason naming the force; pin survives `checkpoint`/`load_checkpoint` round-trip.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T37.md:38:4. Implement eviction regression tests in `budget_tests.rs` (or split): `evict` of a present identity moves it to `excluded_memories` with the caller-supplied reason; `evict` of an absent identity returns Ok with a documented no-op outcome; `evict` does not emit an event when nothing changed (T36 hook respects no-op); `evict` followed by `retrieve` for the same identity re-includes it (eviction is not a permanent deny-list within a session).
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T37.md:39:5. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/checkpoint_tests.rs` covering: `checkpoint` returns a `CheckpointId` whose `load_checkpoint` produces a byte-identical state (verified via canonical-JSON SHA-256); two checkpoints with the same name produce distinct ids and both round-trip; loading an unknown checkpoint id returns a typed error; loading a checkpoint whose `state_version` is newer than the running daemon is rejected with a clear error; restoring a checkpoint resets `selected_memories`, `excluded_memories`, and `budget_decisions` together (they are coherent state, never restored partially).
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T66.md:12:This task ships the cross-language benchmark task fixture repository plus the golden-anchor file that defines the expected retrieval results for every benchmark task. Spec §Phase 9 names "benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors" as a binding deliverable, and spec §Risks "Overfitting To Current Repo" demands the control "fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles." Without cross-language fixtures, every Phase 9 metric measures only Lattice-shaped Rust + TypeScript code and the success-criterion targets in spec §Measurable Success Criteria (30% fewer discovery calls, 40% fewer irrelevant reads, 80% memory precision, 90% relevant-test recommendation) cannot be claimed to generalize.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R25.md:11:This review certifies that the Phase 3 memory graph substrate satisfies every spec invariant before retrieval (Phase 4) starts depending on it. It confirms: the schema covers all 13 memory classes (including `CounterMemory` as a first-class record); links, evidence, accesses, and scores are first-class tables; stream classification and scope enforcement deny-by-default work as specified; the legacy migration imports cleanly and idempotently; CRUD plus the full verification-status state machine emits the right events; contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay all behave correctly. The verdict gates R26 (contract gate) and Phase 4.
