docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T52.md:12:This task enforces the non-negotiable from spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties: "Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency." It implements the label-discipline layer that sits between the verifier (T48–T51) and every consumer that produces a workflow bundle (Phase 4 retrieval, Phase 8 workflow engine, Phase 10 review UI). A memory in `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, or `invalidated` MUST never appear as trusted guidance in any bundle; if surfaced at all, it must carry an unambiguous label and live in a separately-tagged section so an assistant cannot misread it as authoritative. Phase 7 DoD is binding: "stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance."
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T52.md:41:6. Suppress stale memories from ranking promotion: extend the existing ranker signal list (from Phase 4) to include `verification_status` as a hard precondition — only `Verified` enters the trusted bundle's top-N. Non-verified memories may still be returned in the advisory list, ranked separately.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T52.md:43:8. Define the contract surface (`pub trait BundleProducer { fn surface(memories: Vec<Memory>) -> SurfacedBundle; }`) so Phase 4 retrieval (R33-era code) and Phase 8 workflow tools both implement the same interface and cannot diverge.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T52.md:44:9. In `verification/surfacing_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) `Verified` memories land in `trusted`, every other state lands in `advisory` with the matching `BundleSection`, (b) the debug-assertions invariant fires if a test seam injects a `Stale` memory into `trusted`, (c) `label_reason` is non-empty and references the verifier's structured reason payload from T48/T49/T50/T51 (file id, span, scope, expiry), (d) the ranker top-N excludes every non-`Verified` memory regardless of score, (e) `classify` covers all eight `VerificationStatus` variants (exhaustiveness).
