docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T27.md:7:**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine), [§Ranking Complexity](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#ranking-complexity)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T27.md:12:This task delivers the first stage of the Phase 4 retrieval pipeline: a deterministic, feature-based classifier that reads a user task statement and assigns it one or more intent labels (debug, refactor, explain, add-feature, test, docs, migration, performance, review, etc.). The classifier is the input to every later retrieval stage — anchor extraction biases toward intent-relevant token shapes, candidate retrieval weights source mix by intent, and the scorer applies a `task-type compatibility` signal that requires this label to exist. Per spec §Ranking Complexity, the classifier must stay feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy is layered on top, so that ranking remains explainable for the lifetime of Phase 4.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T27.md:20:> "Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: task intent classifier; anchor extractor and resolver; hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events; scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness; compact response shaper; diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons; benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks." (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T27.md:22:> "Risk: ranking becomes hard to reason about. Control: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores." (spec §Ranking Complexity)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T27.md:26:- Dependency outputs: R26 (Phase 3 memory-graph review verdict) — ensures retrieval can rely on typed memory streams, link tables, and verification status fields existing as designed.
