docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T59.md:43:6. Both tools must support compact + full + diagnostic render modes per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles. Compact mode for `verify_explain_memory` returns status + one-line per failed check; diagnostic mode returns the full check trace.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T14.md:12:This task exposes the read side of the event log. It defines a typed query builder for filtering by task, session, workspace, branch, kind, and time range; reconstitutes full `EventEnvelope` values (with payload inlined from either `payload_inline` or the `event_payloads` spillover join); and enforces bounded result sets so a single query can never scan the full log. The reader is the substrate that consolidation (Phase 6), verification (Phase 7), retrieval (Phase 4), and metrics (Phase 9) all sit on. Without it, the event log is write-only.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T14.md:20:> "every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events"
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T23.md:12:This task implements the public memory store API: create, read, update, delete, and the state-machine transitions over `VerificationStatus` (`unverified → in_review → verified`, plus `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`). Writes are idempotent on a caller-provided idempotency key so retries do not double-insert. Every status transition emits the right event via the T13 writer (`MemoryCreated`, `MemoryUpdated`, `MemoryInvalidated`, `MemoryConsolidated`) and records an evidence row pointing at the triggering event. Without this task, the redesigned schema has no callable API and Phase 4 retrieval has nothing to read.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T71.md:12:This task lays the foundation for the entire Phase 10 review surface. It registers a new VS Code webview view under the existing `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` slot in `extension/package.json`, wires a typed JSON-RPC bridge that translates the new memory/event/verification tool responses into UI state, and bootstraps the i18n catalog that every later subview (T72–T76) depends on. Without this scaffolding, the memory inbox, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale view, event trace view, retrieval explanation view, consolidation queue, indexing health, and graph health subviews have no parent panel, no transport, and no string catalog. Spec §10 names the surface explicitly: "This can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product." This task delivers that panel skeleton.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T71.md:16:> "Required operator views: memory inbox; proposed promotions; proposed contradictions; stale memory list; memory evidence view; event trace view; retrieval explanation view; usefulness metrics; workspace graph health; indexing health; consolidation queue."
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T71.md:38:5. Create `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` exporting a typed `ReviewRpcBridge` class wrapping the existing `daemon.ts` client. Expose typed methods for the new tools introduced in Phases 6–9 (memory inbox listing, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale memory, evidence inspection, event trace, retrieval explanation, consolidation queue, indexing health, graph health). Every method returns a `Promise<Result>` shape with explicit error variants — never swallow errors (`coding.md` §Error handling).
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T71.md:74:- All ID inputs use `SearchableSelect` where applicable to webview UI. For VS Code tree-view interactions (e.g. picking a workspace, branch, memory class), use `vscode.window.showQuickPick` with typed entries — never raw `<select>` or `prompt`-based ID entry.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T75.md:1:# T75 — Phase 10 — Event trace view + retrieval explanation view
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T75.md:7:**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§3. Event Log](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#3-event-log), [§7. Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T75.md:12:This task delivers two diagnostic surfaces: the event trace view (operator-facing renderer for the append-only event log filtered by session/task/workspace) and the retrieval explanation view (renders the ranking scores + reasons emitted by the Phase 4 retrieval diagnostic mode). They are the surfaces operators use to answer two binding questions: "what actually happened in this session?" (event trace) and "why did this context get included and that other thing get excluded?" (retrieval explanation). Spec §10 lists both as required; spec §7 requires the ranker to expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode, which is the data shape this UI consumes.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T75.md:16:> "Required operator views: ... event trace view; retrieval explanation view; ..." (spec §10)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T75.md:18:> "The ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result." (spec §7)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T75.md:22:> "New or redesigned memory tools: ... `get_event_trace`." (spec §9)
