docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/results/R55.md:58:2. **`MemoryScope` does not derive `Copy`.** First version of `base_memory` in the support module passed `scope` by value while also consulting it for workspace / branch / org id resolution, which triple-moved the value. Resolved by passing `&MemoryScope` to the helper functions and consuming the value only once into the final `Memory` struct.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/results/R55.md:60:4. **Two `ScopeFilter` types, two `MemoryStore` types in the workspace.** `verification::scope_enforcement::ScopeFilter` is consumed by the legacy `memory::MemoryStore` that the live retrieval pipeline (`retrieval_v1::candidates`) reads through; `memory_graph::scope::ScopeFilter` is the typed-memory store's filter exercised by R25. The R55 gate is deliberately scoped to the path retrieval actually consumes today — the path that the §Non-Negotiable property "Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such" runs through in production. The Findings section of the review documents this scoping decision.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/results/R55.md:61:5. **`retrieval_v1::candidates::retrieve_memory_links` reads via `query_unscoped_admin`.** This is named on the store side as "explicit unscoped path for migrations, snapshots, and legacy admin flows." It is not a contract failure at the surface this gate guards — the §Non-Negotiable property is stamped at `SurfacingPipeline::partition`, which the gate tests directly, and the workflow tools re-apply scope filtering at their own boundary. The review's Findings section calls this out as a Phase 8 integration consideration, not a gate-blocking violation.
